The hard-deny guard no longer refuses a multi-line quoted argument (a commit body, a --body) just because the text mentions a dangerous pattern. Committed as d065ed56 with a Fixes #1101 title. fmt, clippy (-D warnings), build and the full cargo test all pass, and no NEUTERED marker is left.

What changed (`src/hard_deny.rs`): the old code split the command into lines before it tracked quotes. A new logical_lines step now splits only on newlines that are outside quotes. Every multi-line command now gets the same verdict as the same command with its quoted newlines written as spaces. A 21-row table pins this. Each row has a single-line form I typed by hand and an expected verdict I wrote out, so the table can't pass just by agreeing with itself.

• Before the fix: all 14 rows that should refuse already refused. All 7 rows that should pass were refused on multiple lines but passed on one line, which is the bug.
• After: all 21 rows give the expected verdict.

Three things I deliberately don't track quotes in. Tracking them would have opened real holes:
• `#` comments: in # don't / <pattern> / # isn't, the two apostrophes would pair into one quoted word and hide a destructive command that bash really runs.
• Heredoc bodies: their apostrophes would pair up the same way.
• `$'\''`: the escaped quote would be read as a closing quote.

Turning off each of these handlers made exactly its own row fail. Restoring the plain line split made exactly the 7 should-pass rows fail, plus the old test I inverted. Each control was run serially and restored in the same command. I also checked that each passing row's twin, where the pattern actually runs as a command, is still refused.

Cost: a multi-line python3 -c "…" whose inner program runs the pattern now passes. Its one-line form always passed. This is stated in the module doc, the docs page and ARCHITECTURE.md.

One existing test changed: multi_line_quoted_argument_fails_closed asserted the very refusal this removes. I renamed it multi_line_quoted_argument_is_one_word and inverted it, and recorded the old claim as superseded rather than deleting it.

Docs: I updated the module doc (including the old sentence "Parsing quotes across lines is a separate decision, not made here", kept as a superseded quote), the ARCHITECTURE.md entry for hard_deny, and docs/src/configuration/permissions.md.

Left over: heredoc bodies are still read as commands on purpose, because that errs toward refusing. That rule refused two of my own python3 <<'PY' edit scripts while I was making this change. Since this commit closes #1101, I filed #1102 to count those refusals, with "keep it" as my default and a narrower option. I logged both incidents on #1101.
